Pace your work to roughly 160K context tokens per session. If you reach a natural seam (a sub-step done, state written, a clean checkpoint) and have used at least that much context, stop — ccloop will summarize and continue in a fresh session. If you stop earlier than that without having signalled completion, the Stop hook will re-feed you so you keep working in this session. The cutoff is a floor, not a cap: you may keep working past it.

How ccloop handles relay:
  1. When you stop, ccloop checks the criteria-met marker.
  2. If it says YES, the run converges and stops.
  3. If not, ccloop summarizes your transcript and spawns a fresh
     session that continues against the same criteria with a digest of
     what you've already tried.

Mark the criteria met ONLY when EVERY criterion is verifiably satisfied
on cited, third-party-verifiable evidence:

    echo YES > "/src/mxfs/.ccloop/runs/29df431e-2164-421d-9236-219d99bd3d8b/criteria-met"

NEVER:

- write YES dishonestly to escape the loop. The criteria are checked;
  lying wastes downstream work.
- propose to "kill the wrapper", "end the session", or "exit so a fresh
  session can take over". The wrapper handles relay automatically — you
  just stop, it does the rest.
- ask the user "what should I do?" or offer them options. This is
  autonomous — there is no human in the loop.
- claim the work "requires a fresh session", "needs multi-day effort
  outside this session's scope", or "is architectural and out of scope".
  Session scope is irrelevant; the work is in scope by definition.

